---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Workspaces from '../components/Workspaces.astro';
import { url } from '../lib/url';
---

<Base
  title="namesync — terminal workspaces named after what you’re doing"
  description="A herdr plugin that keeps workspace, tab and agent names in step with the work as it changes — and knows when to leave them alone. Works with no model; can use one when you want."
  section="home"
>
  <main>
    <section class="hero">
      <div class="hero__copy">
        <h1 class="hero__title t-display">
          The agent knows what it is working on.
          <em>Your sidebar doesn’t.</em>
        </h1>
        <p class="hero__lede t-lead">
          Spaces get named after directories, because that is all herdr knew when you
          opened them. The agent inside each one has been describing its own work the
          entire time. namesync moves that name onto the space, keeps it current as the
          work changes, and leaves the ones you named yourself alone.
        </p>

        <div class="hero__start">
          <pre class="hero__cmd t-code"><code>herdr plugin install oddurs/herdr-namesync</code></pre>
          <p class="hero__links t-ui">
            <a href={url("docs/install")}>Read the docs</a>
            <a href="https://github.com/oddurs/herdr-namesync">Source</a>
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="hero__demo">
        <Workspaces />
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band">
      <div class="band__inner prose">
        <h2 class="band__heading">Why another renamer</h2>
        <p>
          herdr’s marketplace lists twenty-three plugins that rename things. Almost all
          of them name a session once, from its first prompt, and never revise it. That
          is correct for about ten minutes; by the afternoon the label describes work
          that finished before lunch.
        </p>
        <p>
          That is the problem this plugin was built for, rather than a variation on it.
          Three of them are worth naming, because each is better than namesync at
          something:
        </p>

        <table class="compare">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Instead of</th>
              <th scope="col">Better when</th>
              <th scope="col">namesync when</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">
                <a href="https://github.com/qu8n/herdr-automatic-rename">herdr-automatic-rename</a>
                <span>composes a breadcrumb — <code>api › feat/oauth › nvim</code></span>
              </th>
              <td>You want to know <em>where</em> a pane is: directory, branch, program</td>
              <td>You want to know <em>what is happening</em> in it</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">
                <a href="https://github.com/wyattjoh/herdr-plugin-renamer">herdr-plugin-renamer</a>
                <span>names from the first prompt</span>
              </th>
              <td>One session, one task, short-lived</td>
              <td>A session that runs all day and changes subject</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">
                <a href="https://github.com/iurysza/herdr-tab-smart-rename">herdr-tab-smart-rename</a>
                <span>asks a model per task</span>
              </th>
              <td>You want a model interpreting every task</td>
              <td>You want it free by default, and a model only when it earns it</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>
          The distinction is not what a name is generated from — it is whether anything
          decides when it should change, and whether it can tell you why it left a name
          alone.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band band--rule">
      <div class="band__inner prose">
        <h2 class="band__heading">Nothing generates the name, unless you ask it to</h2>
        <p>
          The work is usually already done. Claude Code, Codex and the rest publish a
          running summary of the current task as their terminal title, and herdr already
          captures it. Ask it yourself:
        </p>
        <pre><code>$ herdr agent list | jq -r '.result.agents[].terminal_title_stripped'
Astro docs site GNU style
Richard Stallman perspective
Open source wifi e-reader</code></pre>
        <p>
          Free, instant, and it works for every agent kind herdr recognises. That is the
          default, and it needs no model, no key and no account.
        </p>
        <p>
          Its one weakness is that agents set a title early and rarely revise it. When a
          title goes stale namesync notices, and can fall back to a model you configure —
          off unless you set an endpoint, consulted only once the free answer has
          demonstrably failed, and held to exactly the same rules as any other name.
          Being expensive buys a name no authority here.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band band--rule">
      <div class="band__inner prose">
        <h2 class="band__heading">When it renames</h2>
        <p>
          herdr emits an event when a pane’s title changes, and deliberately not when
          only the spinner moved. That event is the trigger. Everything after it is the
          part that decides whether to act.
        </p>

        <dl class="rules">
          <div class="rules__row">
            <dt>Your names win</dt>
            <dd>
              A label that isn’t the one namesync last wrote was written by you. It gets
              locked and is never touched again.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div class="rules__row">
            <dt>Defaults are fair game</dt>
            <dd>
              <code>bedreader</code>, <code>w3</code>, <code>tab 2</code>, empty. Nobody
              chose these, so namesync claims them.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div class="rules__row">
            <dt>Rewording isn’t new intent</dt>
            <dd>
              “naming plugin” to “naming plugins” scores 1.00 on a stemmed token overlap
              and is skipped, so the sidebar doesn’t flicker.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div class="rules__row">
            <dt>It waits for you to settle</dt>
            <dd>
              Titles churn early in a turn. A rename holds for a debounce window, then
              at most once per workspace per interval.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div class="rules__row">
            <dt>It stays quiet when you’re stuck</dt>
            <dd>
              While an agent waits on an approval dialog its title describes the
              question, not the work. namesync sits that out and retries.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div class="rules__row">
            <dt>It won’t guess</dt>
            <dd>
              A workspace holding two agents has no single intent, so its tabs get named
              instead and the space is left alone.
            </dd>
          </div>
        </dl>

        <p>
          <code>namesync dry-run</code> prints every decision and the reason behind it,
          and changes nothing. It is the right place to start.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band band--rule">
      <div class="band__inner prose">
        <h2 class="band__heading">Install</h2>
        <pre><code>herdr plugin install oddurs/herdr-namesync
namesync setup --write            # add the sidebar rows herdr needs
herdr integration install claude  # sharper agent state detection</code></pre>
        <p>
          The middle step is the one that catches people out. namesync publishes
          <code>$project</code>, <code>$branch</code>, <code>$since</code> and
          <code>$n</code>, but herdr renders none of them until the sidebar asks — and
          <code>herdr config</code> has no <code>set</code> for a plugin to do it with.
          Without it you install namesync and nothing visible changes.
        </p>
        <p>
          <code>setup</code> prints the rows and writes nothing without
          <code>--write</code>, backs up your config first, and refuses outright if you
          already have a sidebar layout. What it adds is fenced, so
          <code>namesync setup --undo</code> takes it back byte for byte.
        </p>
        <p>Then look before you leap:</p>
        <pre><code>namesync dry-run</code></pre>
        <p>
          Every decision it would make against your live session, with the reason for
          each, and nothing changed. herdr 0.8 or newer, Node 18 or newer, no npm
          dependencies.
        </p>
        <p><a href={url("docs/install")}>Read the docs</a></p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
</Base>

<style>
  main { padding: 0 var(--gutter); }

  .hero {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: clamp(2.5rem, 6vw, 4.5rem);
    align-items: center;
    max-width: var(--page-wide);
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: clamp(3rem, 7vw, 5.5rem) 0 clamp(3rem, 7vw, 5rem);
  }

  @media (min-width: 60rem) {
    .hero { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.05fr) minmax(0, 1fr); }
  }

  /* The italic is the rhetorical turn of the sentence, not an accent on a
     word: the first clause states, the second undercuts it. */
  /* Type comes from .t-display; this only positions it. The negative margin
     pulls the cap of the T back to the optical margin so the headline aligns
     with the lede below it. */
  .hero__title {
    margin: 0 0 0.62em -0.028em;
  }

  /* The turn in the sentence. IBM Plex Sans has a true italic — drawn, not
     slanted — so it reads as a change of voice rather than a tilt. */
  .hero__title em {
    display: block;
    font-style: italic;
    font-weight: var(--weight-regular);
    letter-spacing: -0.019em;
    color: var(--fg-strong);
  }

  .hero__lede {
    max-width: 27rem;
  }

  /* A copyable command rather than a button. The audience installs plugins from
     a terminal all day; a call-to-action pill would be the one element on the
     page borrowed from a different kind of site. */
  .hero__start { margin-top: 1.9em; }

  /* Same surface as a prose code block, so it reads as one element the site
     already uses rather than a hero-only invention. */
  .hero__cmd {
    margin: 0;
    max-width: 27rem;
    padding: var(--space-4) var(--space-5);
    background: var(--bg-sunken);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
    overflow-x: auto;
  }

  .hero__links {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--space-5);
    margin: 1.05em 0 0;
  }

  .band { padding: clamp(3rem, 7vw, 4.75rem) 0; }
  .band--rule { border-top: 1px solid var(--border); }

  /* One left spine for the whole page: the reading column starts at the same
     edge as the headline instead of being centred, so the eye never has to
     re-find where the text begins. */
  .band__inner {
    max-width: var(--page-wide);
    margin: 0 auto;
  }

  .band__inner > * {
    max-width: min(var(--measure), 100%);
  }

  /* These sections already sit behind a full-width rule, so the heading drops
     the one the prose styles add. */
  .band__heading {
    margin-top: 0;
    padding-top: 0;
    border-top: 0;
  }

  /* Wider than the reading measure: three columns of prose do not fit in 32rem,
     and this is the one element on the page that earns the extra width. */
  .band__inner > .compare { max-width: 100%; }

  .compare { margin: 1.8em 0; }

  /* prose.css styles `tbody td`, not a row header, so this cell would otherwise
     sit flush against the rule with no padding at all. */
  .compare tbody th[scope='row'] {
    text-align: left;
    padding: 0.75em 1.1em 0.75em 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
    vertical-align: baseline;
    color: var(--fg-strong);
    font-weight: var(--weight-regular);
  }

  .compare tbody tr:last-child th[scope='row'] { border-bottom: 0; }

  .compare th[scope='row'] a { font-weight: var(--weight-semi); }

  /* The concession, set quieter than the name it qualifies. */
  .compare th[scope='row'] span {
    display: block;
    margin-top: 0.2em;
    font-weight: var(--weight-regular);
    color: var(--fg-muted);
  }

  /* Stacked on a phone: three columns of prose in 22rem is four words a line.
     Each row becomes its own block, with the two verdicts labelled. */
  @media (max-width: 46rem) {
    .compare, .compare tbody, .compare tr, .compare td { display: block; }
    .compare thead { display: none; }

    .compare tr {
      padding: 1.05em 0;
      border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
    }

    .compare tr:last-child { border-bottom: 0; }

    .compare th[scope='row'] {
      display: block;
      padding: 0 0 0.5em;
      border: 0;
    }

    .compare td {
      padding: 0.25em 0 0 1.1em;
      border: 0;
      position: relative;
    }

    .compare td::before {
      content: '›';
      position: absolute;
      left: 0;
      color: var(--accent);
    }
  }

  .rules { margin: 1.8em 0; }

  .rules__row {
    padding: 1.05em 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
  }

  .rules__row:last-child { border-bottom: 0; }

  /* Label in sans, description in serif: the same split as the rest of the
     site, at the scale of one row. */
  .rules dt {
    font-weight: var(--weight-semi);
    color: var(--fg-strong);
    margin-bottom: 0.3em;
  }

  .rules dd { color: var(--fg-muted); }

  @media (min-width: 44rem) {
    .rules__row {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: 12rem minmax(0, 1fr);
      gap: var(--space-5);
      align-items: baseline;
    }

    .rules dt { margin-bottom: 0; }
  }
</style>
